a massive fire is still burning in  southern Texas after an explosion at a  chemical plant a second blast rattled  the city of Port Neches yesterday  afternoon the initial explosion hit  early yesterday morning and was felt up  to 30 miles away tens of thousands of  people have been told to evacuate Omar  Villafranca is in Texas with a look at  the devastation a wall of flames erupted  from the tpc plant in Port Neches Texas  just hours after a massive blast tore  through the refinery early Wednesday  morning the new explosion forced a  mandatory evacuation of about 60,000  people within a four-mile radius of the  plant putting many residents  Thanksgiving plans on hold I made in the  freezer ready to cook I had a turkey in  the oven just lit up the whole entire  sky hadn't seen anything like that  before the first blast happened just  after midnight in an area of the plant  that produces a highly flammable  chemical used to make synthetic rubber  and other products officials say it was  felt up to 30 miles away and blew out  windows and doors of nearby homes three  workers were hurt during the explosion  all have been treated and released state  authorities say a series of smaller  explosions occurred throughout the day  it puts a lot of fear and a lot of  families because their husbands and  wives and children work in these  refineries Texas has already seen  massive fires in at least five chemical  plants this year in April a fire at a  plant in Crosby just northeast of  Houston killed one person and seriously  injured two a loss of power at the plant  is preventing investigators from  estimating how long the fire will last  it's horrific when you realize what  force took the front door and put it in  your living room you know we're thankful  we're all safe we have a lot to be  thankful for this Thanksgiving for CBS  this morning I'm Omar Villafranca
 

